  "music": "w4-castle-clatter",
  "authorNotes": "Solo. Two new mechanics escalating together: a second lever, and a signalRelay chain that turns 'both levers, within a couple of seconds' into something one body can actually do. Wiring, in order: lever-w4-06-a (non-latching, on the starting ledge) fires 'w4-06-lever-a' only while held; relay-w4-06-hold-a (op delay, delayMs 3200) reads that and holds 'w4-06-a-held' true for 3.2s after the lever is released — long enough to let go, cross swing-w4-06-link (250px, the ledges are only 250px apart on purpose) and grab lever-w4-06-b on the far ledge. relay-w4-06-and (op and) is true only while both 'w4-06-a-held' and lever B's raw signal are true together, which produces 'w4-06-ab-momentary' for roughly the width of that overlap. relay-w4-06-hold-gate (op delay, 2000ms) stretches that momentary coincidence into a 2s window on 'w4-06-gate', which door-w4-06 consumes — long enough to let go of lever B and dash through door-w4-06 before it drops again. Past the gate, swing-w4-06-final (290px, the widest single swing the world had shown up to this point) carries the player to the third pier (1550-2450) — the checkpoint sits here (1700,760), on solid ground just after the gate, so a botched landing doesn't send a solved puzzle back to square one. That third pier was originally mislabelled as the goal pier while the goal itself sat 300px further out; swing-w4-06-goal (pivot 2430,520, 280px) now closes that last gap to the actual goal pier (2700-3300, holding the 2750-2900 goal shape). The previous checkpoint coordinate (1300,760) also sat in mid-air past the second pier's edge rather than on it — moved onto solid ground as part of the same pass."
